WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.550
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.480
You took me by the hand.

9
00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:49.790
I have tears in my eyes because I am so excited.

10
00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.670
You know what that song means.

11
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.920
It's time for a dinner party.

12
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:57.580
Oh, my gosh.

13
00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.820
I'm so excited.

14
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.680
You and I watched this episode so many times over and over this week.

15
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.000
And I'm telling you, I loved it every single time.

16
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.200
Angela, my husband Lee had never seen this episode.

17
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.720
How is that possible?

18
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:13.900
That is crazy.

19
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.380
I got to watch it with a person who's never seen it.

20
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.940
I thought he was going to choke.

21
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.500
He was laughing so hard, especially during the condo tour.

22
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.020
He couldn't catch his breath.

23
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.400
Well, my whole family had to watch it over and over.

24
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.240
Each one of them watched it with me once.

25
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:39.240
Guys, it is season four, episode 13, written by Gene Stepnitsky and Lee Eisenberg, directed by Paul Feig.

26
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.660
Arguably, maybe the best episode of The Office.

27
00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.080
Definitely one of the most memorable.

28
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:54.280
Every time Jenna and I are, you know, doing interviews or talking about the show, this is an episode we are always asked about.

29
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.980
I feel so honored that I got to be at that awkward dinner table.

30
00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.060
What a great lifelong memory.

31
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.340
And we have some really special things for you today.

32
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:07.300
Starting with the summary.

33
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.220
I'm not reading the summary today, Angela.

34
00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.760
Oh, no, no, no, no.

35
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.780
You know who needs to read the summary.

36
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.800
The host of the dinner party himself.

37
00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:19.500
Mr. Steve Carell.

38
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:26.620
You are cordially invited to Michael and Jan's condo for a last-minute, couples-only dinner party.

39
00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.220
Attendance is limited.

40
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.580
We only have six wine glasses.

41
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:36.460
Join Jim and Pam, Angela and Andy, and eventually Dwight and his former babysitter

42
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:42.960
for an evening of drinks, games, music, candles, Osso Bucco, and a home tour like no other.

43
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:44.600
Snip, snap, snip, snap.

44
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.940
Let's get to it.

45
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:46.940
Yeah!

46
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.080
Ralph, we're reading our summary.

47
00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:56.540
So, guys, we were a little nervous about this one because it is such a big episode.

48
00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.780
We wanted to really do a good job.

49
00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:05.880
And there is so much to cover, so Jenna and I reached out to all of the guests of the dinner party

50
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.380
and also to our writers and our director, Paul Feig.

51
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.940
And everyone is sending in audio clips.

52
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:13.240
Let's get started.

53
00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:19.080
Fast fact number one, this episode was written by Gene Stupnitsky and Lee Eisenberg.

54
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.240
I spoke with both of them, Angela.

55
00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:27.260
Gene told me that the inspiration for this episode was the Edward Albee play,

56
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.180
Who's Afraid of Virginia Woolf?

57
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.300
Now, if you guys don't know this play, it is amazing.

58
00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:40.800
And it is about a crazy older married couple who invites a younger couple from their work over for a dinner party.

59
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.580
And it's just this insane evening.

60
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.480
It completely goes off the rails.

61
00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.160
There's a movie version starring Elizabeth Taylor and Richard Burton that is crazy good.

62
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:52.020
You can check it out.

63
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.800
If you liked this episode, you'll like that movie.

64
00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.180
Here's a little fun fact from Lee Eisenberg.

65
00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:01.520
The original working title for this episode was Who's Afraid of Jan Levinson Gould?

66
00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:08.560
Which makes total sense to me because Jan Levinson Gould really brings it.

67
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.640
She scared the bejesus out of me in this episode.

68
00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.960
There are a few moments where she looks at Pam where I was like, I would not want to be Pam today.

69
00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:18.360
For sure.

70
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:27.640
Gene told me that they were assigned this script right before the writers went on their summer hiatus in June of 2007.

71
00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:33.800
So, guys, we would go on this little hiatus every summer and the writers could go off and write episodes.

72
00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.480
Normally, writers only got one week to write an episode.

73
00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:44.780
But because they were on hiatus, they had three weeks to work on this episode.

74
00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.880
I mean, they only got paid for one.

75
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.680
But they said they couldn't put it down.

76
00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:50.680
They just kept adding details and details.

77
00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.500
They used their whole hiatus to write it.

78
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.540
But, I mean, I think it shows.

79
00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.800
I don't want to change the whole structure of how we do television.

80
00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.520
Maybe we should all get three weeks for a script.

81
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:01.120
Just saying.

82
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.760
So, we had a fan question from Megan W.

83
00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:04.880
and Meredith H.

84
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.760
who want to know what everyone's initial reaction to this episode was when we did the table read.

85
00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:11.860
Oh, guys.

86
00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:14.760
One of the first people I reached out to was Melora.

87
00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.160
And she responded immediately.

88
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.200
Here is Melora's reaction to getting the script.

89
00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.340
When I read the dinner party episode, I was definitely just blown away.

90
00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:31.040
I was on the floor laughing and really felt like it was going to be so much fun.

91
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.760
I just couldn't wait to sink my teeth into it.

92
00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:46.160
It just felt like, you know, all of the wonderful, unhealthy, and completely dysfunctional elements of Jan and Michael kind of coming to a head.

93
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.520
And I couldn't really wait to dive in and get going.

94
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:56.860
Angela, Gene told me that during the table read of this, he sweated through his shirt.

95
00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:03.740
He said it started off a little quiet because the episode is pretty dark.

96
00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:12.180
But that by the end, when the cop says, not now, Dwight, the whole room just erupted into laughter.

97
00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:18.840
And afterwards, Greg Daniels came up to him and Lee and said, this script was perfect.

98
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:19.320
Wow.

99
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:30.020
So Gene reminded me that before he and Lee got their jobs on the office, Gene had been working part-time as Harold Ramis' nanny.

100
00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.260
And Lee was a temp at HBO.

101
00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.920
And he just said, Greg changed our lives.

102
00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:44.480
So hearing from this man who was our mentor telling us we nailed it, it was the highest compliment he ever received as a writer.

103
00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:47.120
I bet that means so much to them.

104
00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:50.560
You know, Jenna, here's what I remember about this table read.

105
00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.040
I do remember this build.

106
00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.200
It started and it just took over the room.

107
00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.600
People were laughing and then laughing and then really laughing.

108
00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:07.620
And by the end, that final line of the script, everyone started applauding.

109
00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.700
And I remember the applause lasted for like a minute.

110
00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:11.480
Yeah.

111
00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:19.640
It must have felt so amazing for Gene and Lee to see their hard work, just have that kind of adoration in the moment.

112
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.380
Well, get this, guys.

113
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:26.820
I guess the folks at NBC did not love this script.

114
00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.020
Oh, no!

115
00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.800
After the table read, Greg had a phone call with NBC.

116
00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:33.940
Uh-oh.

117
00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:38.500
Well, when we asked Rainn Wilson about this episode, he talks about that phone call.

118
00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:40.420
Dinner Party.

119
00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.100
It is one of the top three all-time great office episodes.

120
00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.680
It's absolutely extraordinary.

121
00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:50.720
It's a pretty dark episode.

122
00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:56.460
And after the table read, I heard a story from Greg Daniels that the network was like,

123
00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:59.180
um, that's a very dark episode.

124
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:00.820
And Greg was like, yes.

125
00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:02.100
Yes, it is.

126
00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.500
They're like, are we sure we want it so dark?

127
00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.440
And Greg was like, yep.

128
00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.320
And they were like, okay.

129
00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.580
And then we proceeded.

130
00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:19.660
You know, I feel like if this episode had come earlier in our run, maybe it wouldn't have happened, right?

131
00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:24.540
Maybe NBC would have given notes and Greg would have maybe had to sort of pick his battles.

132
00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:29.620
But at this point, our show was so established and critically acclaimed and had won awards.

133
00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:35.680
I feel like Greg was able to confidently say to them, like, yeah, this is the show.

134
00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:36.580
This is how it's written.

135
00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:37.560
And we're going to do it.

136
00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:42.040
Jean and Lee told me that they did not do a rewrite on the script.

137
00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:48.300
Wow, you guys, all of our episodes, as amazing as they were, would go through several rewrites.

138
00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:51.000
That's just kind of how a script comes to be.

139
00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.860
So for this to not have a single rewrite, that is amazing.

140
00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:56.780
Yeah.

141
00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.140
So we had this amazing script.

142
00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.560
We're all ready to shoot this amazing episode.

143
00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:06.920
And then we got shut down because of the writer's strike.

144
00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:07.880
Yeah.

145
00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.500
For four months, we had to wait.

146
00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:18.840
It was just a very surreal time because so many shows that shut down did not come back.

147
00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.480
They weren't able to survive the strike.

148
00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:26.580
And the fact that we were able to come back and we knew we had scripts waiting for us was huge.

149
00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:31.920
But it was really important for us as a cast that we stand with our writers and support them.

150
00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:41.720
Jean and Lee said it was really surreal because they went from writing this episode, having this table read, to picketing their own set.

151
00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:42.240
Yeah.

152
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.260
And it was so, so bizarre.

153
00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.460
But we shut down.

154
00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:48.220
This leads me to fast fact number two.

155
00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.060
Mm-hmm.

156
00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:57.680
Fast fact number two is that Paul Feig directed this episode, but he almost did not direct this episode.

157
00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.040
I couldn't even imagine if it wasn't him.

158
00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:01.280
I know.

159
00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.140
I know.

160
00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:07.100
Well, when we reached out to him, he told us how this came about.

161
00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.980
I had been brought on.

162
00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.480
I directed a lot of episodes of The Office.

163
00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:20.080
But in that season, I had been given Survivorman, which I was really excited about and actually very, very proud of that episode.

164
00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:25.560
But I remember reading the script for the dinner party and it was given to some other director.

165
00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:31.420
And I was so jealous because I thought, like, if only I could do this episode, this is like everything I want to do.

166
00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:34.580
It's so uncomfortable and cringy and hilarious.

167
00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.000
But another director was assigned to it.

168
00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:38.920
But then the writer's strike happened.

169
00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:42.040
And so it all fell apart.

170
00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:45.300
Because I did Survivorman and then the writer's strike stopped everything.

171
00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.080
And so we were off for months and months and months, however long the writer's strike went.

172
00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:57.740
And my great joy was at the end of that, everything shifted so much that that director who was supposed to do the dinner party wasn't able to.

173
00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.500
And so it came back around to me.

174
00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:03.400
And so I was so excited to do that episode.

175
00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:05.620
I just saw it so clearly in my head.

176
00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.460
And it was just so funny to me.

177
00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:13.000
But the crazy thing about that episode was once we did it, we knew it was so funny.

178
00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:17.580
The audience, the loyal office audiences hated it.

179
00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:26.780
It was really a very divisive episode because I think it was so uncomfortable and so cringy that people couldn't deal with it.

180
00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:30.340
And I remember we were so excited, all of us, all the writers, everybody,

181
00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:34.300
we were so excited because we really thought this was kind of like one of the best things the show could do.

182
00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:38.320
And we were kind of all bummed out that people were so weird about it.

183
00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:44.020
But I think the whole thing was it was so uncomfortable that the first time you watch it, you almost can't handle it.

184
00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:50.060
But once you watch it, a second, a third, and fourth, and however many times you watch it, then it becomes fun because you know it's coming.

185
00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:51.540
You know it's going to be terrible.

186
00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.600
And you just can enjoy the cringiness.

187
00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:57.120
And so I could not be prouder of that episode.

188
00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:02.660
It's really, you know, I won a DGA directing award for it, which is the only time I'll ever get an award for anything.

189
00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:05.120
But I just think it's the funniest thing in the world.

190
00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:14.420
I remember the fan response to this episode was not overwhelmingly positive when the show first aired.

191
00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:20.820
Yeah, I remember even within my own extended family, they either loved it or they hated it.

192
00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.160
But, yes, very strong reactions.

193
00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:25.300
It has grown to be beloved.

194
00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.440
It was not beloved when it first aired.

195
00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:32.920
You know, I don't know who this other director was that was supposed to direct this episode.

196
00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:38.280
But Gene told me that this person had never directed a single camera comedy ever.

197
00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.160
That this episode would have been their first one.

198
00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:42.460
Oh, my Lord.

199
00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:49.280
Now, maybe it was a big-time movie person, you know, and they'd just never done half-hour TV before.

200
00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.380
I am so glad that we got Paul Feig.

201
00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:53.360
Yeah.

202
00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.680
I mean, you don't want the dinner party script to be your first time at single camera comedy.

203
00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:00.900
If I were that person, I wouldn't sleep.

204
00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:01.500
Yeah.

205
00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:02.620
No.

206
00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:08.520
Fast fact number three, Angela, we know that the writer's strike provided us with Paul Feig.

207
00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:13.220
It also provided us with a problem in the form of your giant belly.

208
00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:16.400
I was wondering when you were going to bring that up.

209
00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:24.180
I'm not the only person bringing up your belly, Angela, because when we asked Paul Feig about his memories of shooting this episode, he discussed it.

210
00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:25.480
Oh, no.

211
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:35.380
One of my favorites is how we had to desperately hide Angela's pregnancy during it because she was very late, late stages.

212
00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:43.960
And so you'll notice a lot of her carrying flowers and things in the foreground and then a pillow on her lap when she's sitting on the couch.

213
00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:46.400
I think we had her in a big, giant overcoat, too.

214
00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:47.960
So that was fun.

215
00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:50.940
It was very challenging, doing special effects with no special effects.

216
00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:59.700
So, Angela, we also got mail about your belly from Susie S., Alex G., and Emma O.

217
00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.060
Oh, no, ladies.

218
00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:10.580
They want to know how far along were you in your pregnancy and what kind of meeting was there for hiding Angela's baby bump in this episode?

219
00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:11.780
Please tell us everything.

220
00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:17.000
Well, ladies, if memory serves me, we filmed this episode in early March.

221
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.280
Now, you have to remember, when we did the deposition, I was barely showing.

222
00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:24.080
Four months have gone by into my pregnancy.

223
00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.260
I had my baby early May.

224
00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:29.100
So it was a belly.

225
00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.560
It was a full-on baby belly.

226
00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.020
And I do remember poor Paul Feig.

227
00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:38.260
He's the sweetest person coming up to me and being like,

228
00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:42.800
Hey, Angela, so we're going to have you stand behind the copier.

229
00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:44.420
We're going to have you hold this pillow.

230
00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.400
We're going to have you hold a purse.

231
00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.220
We're going to have flowers.

232
00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:47.120
We're going to have a wall.

233
00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:47.780
You stand behind.

234
00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.440
There were like all of these things.

235
00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:52.980
There were actual meetings about how to hide my belly.

236
00:14:53.180 --> 00:15:01.520
And then when we started filming, I remember tucking behind the copier and Matt Sohn, our camera operator, saying,

237
00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:03.220
I can still see it, Ange.

238
00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.060
I was like, what?

239
00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:09.060
So now there was this new dialogue I had with the camera operators where they'd be like,

240
00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:13.660
back it up a little, pivot, pivot, duck, hold it higher.

241
00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:16.560
We had this whole language of the belly hide.

242
00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:22.880
Am I correct that in that scene when you first enter the condo and Michael and Jan are trying to take your coats,

243
00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.300
you don't take off your coat because you needed it to hide your belly?

244
00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:27.220
Oh, yeah.

245
00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.140
And my coat was enormous.

246
00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:30.920
It's like not a coat I would ever wear.

247
00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:32.680
It was so big.

248
00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:38.800
But yeah, I have a lot of track the belly moments for this episode that I can sprinkle in as we go.

249
00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:40.120
But we had to get creative.

250
00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:41.300
It was a big belly.

251
00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:42.620
One day, no belly.

252
00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:43.640
Next day, big belly.

253
00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:45.660
That's what happened during the writer's strike.

254
00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:48.240
Well, guys, those are my fast facts.

255
00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:55.460
I think we should take a break and we will be back with a dinner party and more audio clips from our dinner guests.

256
00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:56.720
Light your candles.

257
00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:58.360
Get your osso buco going.

258
00:15:58.680 --> 00:15:59.960
We're about to have a dinner party.

259
00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:12.220
We are back.

260
00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.660
We are at the office.

261
00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:15.340
It's a Friday night.

262
00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:16.880
We're working late.

263
00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:23.600
Oh, there's a special assignment from corporate, which we never find out what the special assignment is.

264
00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:30.220
Like, really and truly, how creative did Michael Scott have to get that he was able to con the whole bullpen?

265
00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:32.700
Yeah, everyone's shuffling papers.

266
00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:34.360
They're looking at things.

267
00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:35.520
What are we doing?

268
00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:37.580
What do we think the assignment is?

269
00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.600
It seems like we're doing stuff.

270
00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:46.460
I feel like this is Michael's smartest moment that he came up with a special assignment that everyone believed.

271
00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:51.760
At 36 seconds, Jenna, is when Jim knows.

272
00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:56.860
I believe this is when Jim knows because Michael gets on the phone to corporate and he's like,

273
00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:59.180
it's not fair to these people.

274
00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:02.580
These people are my friends and I care about them.

275
00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:05.160
Like, who talks like that to corporate?

276
00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:06.140
I know.

277
00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:07.000
Jim knows.

278
00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:08.620
But people are grumbling.

279
00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:09.660
They're griping.

280
00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.340
And there is a great deleted scene.

281
00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.360
It's a whole runner with Toby.

282
00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:20.100
Toby is sharing that he's so bummed out because he had tickets to Tom Petty.

283
00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:21.120
What?

284
00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:21.780
Yeah.

285
00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:26.940
And Toby had to give them to his ex-wife and her new boyfriend.

286
00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:28.600
Oh, no.

287
00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:33.580
So, yeah, everyone is kind of like, they're bummed out.

288
00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:35.020
Why are they staying so late?

289
00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.340
And then Michael proudly walks in because he's had this phone call.

290
00:17:38.420 --> 00:17:39.180
And he's like, you know what?

291
00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:40.520
You don't have to stay late.

292
00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.480
I told corporate we're not doing it.

293
00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:43.900
Everyone can go home.

294
00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:46.340
And they're like, wow, thank you, Michael.

295
00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:47.240
Yeah.

296
00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:54.240
And then he turns to Jim and he says, hey, you want to come have dinner at our place tonight?

297
00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:56.820
I know you have no plans.

298
00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:57.800
Oh.

299
00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.500
And Jim's like, you got me.

300
00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:00.180
Yep.

301
00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:00.580
Yeah.

302
00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:05.380
Well, there's a series of deleted talking heads where people talk about their ideal dinner party.

303
00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:09.880
And there's one by Michael Scott that really cracked me up.

304
00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:11.140
Sam, can we play that?

305
00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:13.460
My ideal dinner party.

306
00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:13.900
Easy.

307
00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:22.780
Jim Pam, Ryan, the mayor, Barack Obama, Shiloh Jolie-Pitt, because at the end of the night,

308
00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:27.080
Brad or Angelina would have to come and pick him up and I would get to meet them.

309
00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:30.380
Shiloh Beef, because of Disturbia.

310
00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:35.120
All of the children of the world.

311
00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.260
Val Kilmer, but he probably wouldn't come.

312
00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:40.020
Too famous.

313
00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.080
Obviously, George Clooney.

314
00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:47.980
And Jan, definitely.

315
00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:49.160
If there was room.

316
00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.720
Michael would invite all of the children of the world.

317
00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.640
Val Kilmer, definitely George Clooney.

318
00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:58.640
But only Jan if there was room.

319
00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:01.380
Oh, that tells you everything you need to know.

320
00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:01.840
Right?

321
00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:06.300
And I feel like they deleted that because it was tipping his hand too soon.

322
00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:06.880
Right?

323
00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:14.740
Well, now we have a very sad talking head from Dwight that ends with just crying.

324
00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:17.260
He's so sad he hasn't been invited.

325
00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:17.860
Yeah.

326
00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:21.340
I thought Rain was so funny in this talking head.

327
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:22.620
He really made me laugh.

328
00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:23.400
Bless his heart.

329
00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:24.580
All right, lady.

330
00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.240
It's time to go to the condo.

331
00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:26.860
All right.

332
00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:29.360
So Pam and Jim arrive to the condo.

333
00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:35.020
It's already awkward and this is going to be a comedy sprint to the end.

334
00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.220
It's just going to be one joke after the next.

335
00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:44.600
Well, it starts with both Michael and Jan trying to hug Jim at the same time.

336
00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:48.060
And then I don't know if you noticed, but only Michael hugs Pam.

337
00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:50.420
Jan does not even go in for a hug.

338
00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:51.300
Oh, I noticed.

339
00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:55.280
And then she throws you some serious shade about the wine you brought.

340
00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:57.360
She's like, oh, thank you so much.

341
00:19:57.360 --> 00:20:01.640
This will be great to cook with, which you guys know is code for thanks for the crappy

342
00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:02.100
wine.

343
00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:03.280
Exactly.

344
00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:11.300
Angela, Jean told me that one of their goals in this episode was that it would just be loaded

345
00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:16.220
with a ton of subtle passive aggressive comments and coded language.

346
00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:20.500
And that this dig at Pam about the wine was the firSt.

347
00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:27.340
Jean also told me that this moment was inspired by the fact that he and Lee were roommates.

348
00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:33.000
When they first started writing on the show and that Lee Eisenberg is an amazing cook.

349
00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:34.840
He referred to him as a gourmand.

350
00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:35.740
What?

351
00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:36.700
Yeah.

352
00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:41.900
And one time, Jean saw Lee cooking with some wine, which was something that Jean did not

353
00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:42.680
know you could do.

354
00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:45.660
His actual words were, it blew my mind.

355
00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:47.120
He's such a dude.

356
00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:49.380
Of course you can cook with wine.

357
00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:53.760
Well, I guess Lee explained to him that it has to be a cheap wine.

358
00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:57.160
And that always stuck in his head that you cook with cheap wine.

359
00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:59.460
And that's why he gave that line to Jan.

360
00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:00.840
Well, it's perfect.

361
00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:03.340
Babe, should we take a tour, babe?

362
00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:04.260
Yeah, babe.

363
00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:07.000
Oh, I'm so sorry about this horrendous carpet.

364
00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.220
We're a work in progress.

365
00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.520
I call this the mask wall, babe.

366
00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.860
Oh, we watch this episode a lot, but it's so good.

367
00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:23.100
This home tour really kicks off all of the Jan and Michael dynamics in this episode.

368
00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:29.460
And when we asked John Krasinski about his memories of this episode, he talked about this

369
00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:32.860
scene of Jim and Pam arriving and then starting the tour.

370
00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:38.160
That episode was without a doubt one of my favorite to ever shoot because there was this

371
00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:42.460
incredible energy on set, this idea that we were finally getting to pull the curtain

372
00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:44.560
back on the world of Michael Scott.

373
00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:51.060
And I think that for me, I was already almost laughing in every scene because of how Steve

374
00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.720
was playing his character.

375
00:21:52.720 --> 00:22:01.880
There was this unbelievable beaming of pride and joy to have these two people finally in

376
00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:09.280
his home after what I imagine to be a lifetime of hoping and wishing that it could be possible

377
00:22:09.280 --> 00:22:09.740
one day.

378
00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:10.700
And here they were.

379
00:22:10.900 --> 00:22:14.780
And not only were they in his home, he was able to give them a tour.

380
00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:22.620
And then the battling dichotomy of Michael's excitement and Jan's sort of weighty judgment

381
00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:27.500
of his house and his excitement was all loaded into every scene.

382
00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.040
It's so true.

383
00:22:30.340 --> 00:22:30.840
So true.

384
00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:32.060
So well said, JK.

385
00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:37.920
We got a fan question, Angela, from Cameron W., Linda M., and Monica J.

386
00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:41.640
Did you guys actually film in someone's house?

387
00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:42.900
Oh, yeah.

388
00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:45.180
Yeah, there was a man that owned this condo.

389
00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:49.040
Yeah, it was a condo in Woodland Hills, California.

390
00:22:49.340 --> 00:22:55.800
And I remember they told me they had to take out all of that man's furniture and repaint his

391
00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:56.240
walls.

392
00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:00.260
And then they had to put it all back the way he had it.

393
00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:01.680
And that blew my mind.

394
00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:02.720
I know.

395
00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:07.000
In the deposition DVD commentary, Ed talked about this for a minute.

396
00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:12.220
He said when they filmed in the garage, this man's belongings, there were some of them

397
00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:14.700
that they just shoved all into one corner.

398
00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:18.060
And they were like, what is this guy about?

399
00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:19.560
Because guess what he had?

400
00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:20.180
What?

401
00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:24.400
He had all of these professional ping pong player posters.

402
00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:25.440
What?

403
00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:26.400
Yes.

404
00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.240
And they had just had all this ping pong in the deposition.

405
00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:30.600
So they were like, oh, my gosh.

406
00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:33.580
And they recognized some of the names because of Dwight's talking head.

407
00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:40.120
And he said, in addition to all the professional ping pong posters, he had a professional ping

408
00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:43.660
pong table that was folded up and sort of wedged in the corner.

409
00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:44.740
Wow.

410
00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:47.960
So this guy obviously was a ping pong enthusiaSt.

411
00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:49.020
We know that.

412
00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:52.800
Maybe he was an amateur uprising professional ping pong player.

413
00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:54.280
Maybe he was a champion.

414
00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:55.000
We don't know.

415
00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:55.840
We don't know.

416
00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:00.500
But yes, he had to move all of his stuff out and cram a bunch of stuff into the corner

417
00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:01.120
of the garage.

418
00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:03.420
And then we had to put it all back for him.

419
00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:09.460
Well, we asked Paul Feig about designing the condo for Michael and Jan.

420
00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:11.520
And here is what he had to say.

421
00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:18.700
For Michael and Jan's condo, I had had the benefit of being the person, the director who

422
00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:23.940
got to do Office Olympics, which was where we established Michael and Jan's condo when

423
00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:24.600
he bought it.

424
00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:31.360
And so, you know, found this very kind of stark, normal, cookie cutter kind of condo that he

425
00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.620
would have with very stark white walls and all that.

426
00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:34.860
And that was really fun.

427
00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:40.180
And so when I was lucky enough to get to do this episode and go back to there, the whole

428
00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:46.080
concept of what Jan would have done with that place was really fun to play with.

429
00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:49.960
And I remember Lee and Jean and I got really hung up on the idea that she had a warhol.

430
00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:54.860
I think they even put it into the script, but that she had done a like a warhol-esque print

431
00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:58.080
of herself, which you only see briefly when they're in the hallway.

432
00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:03.480
I think when Jim and Pam first arrive and you see it in the stairwell when they go up to

433
00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:11.140
the infamous candle room and it's like this quad warhol style picture of Jan.

434
00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:16.900
But I just love the idea that we had to show off how Jan had fixed up the place, but also

435
00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:21.260
the idea that Jan had done it, even though she tried to warm it up, it was kind of very

436
00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:21.720
cold.

437
00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:27.420
So we were hung up on the idea of like really like dark gray walls and what art she would

438
00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:27.900
have put up.

439
00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:32.700
And I was very, very hung up on the idea of them putting up a, that Jan put up a kimono

440
00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:37.260
on the wall, which I had seen somewhere and thought, oh, that's kind of a cool decoration,

441
00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:41.340
but it's also not terribly warm at the same time.

442
00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:42.680
And so that made me laugh.

443
00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:50.280
I have to add another little tidbit, Angela, is that I guess they put up a bunch of photos.

444
00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:56.100
You can't really tell when you're watching, but they are all photos of Jan and other men.

445
00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:58.840
There are no photos of Jan and Michael.

446
00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:03.020
And then there's a lot of photos I remember of just Jan by herself.

447
00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:03.980
Yes.

448
00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:07.540
Like in a frame, like she has made it the Jan shrine.

449
00:26:09.560 --> 00:26:15.280
But I love that she has given it her touch, but it's in no way homey.

450
00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:17.920
No, it's just really kind of cold.

451
00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:22.720
And Michael has no space that is his own, except maybe where all of his rejected furniture

452
00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.420
items have gone into the garage.

453
00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:25.200
Yeah.

454
00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:27.940
They take Pam and Jim on this tour.

455
00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:33.780
And we learned that Jan has both an office space and a workspace because she can't create

456
00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:35.660
in her office space.

457
00:26:35.660 --> 00:26:39.520
And her desk and her office space look like no one had ever sat there.

458
00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:40.740
It's so sparse.

459
00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:46.960
Oh, at three minutes, 32 seconds, you can see that her desk chair still has the plastic cover

460
00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:47.680
on the arMs.

461
00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:48.780
Oh my gosh.

462
00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:49.340
Fantastic.

463
00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:52.120
That was a little detail that Paul Feig had.

464
00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:53.240
He thought that would be funny.

465
00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:54.860
So he made them leave it on.

466
00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:55.900
That's great.

467
00:26:56.280 --> 00:27:00.120
They put so much thought into every little nook and cranny of that condo.

468
00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:02.160
Should we talk about the candle room?

469
00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:03.620
Yes.

470
00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:07.120
I can't imagine what that smelled like.

471
00:27:07.240 --> 00:27:09.040
It must have been so strong.

472
00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:10.160
Oh, lady.

473
00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:12.040
We got a lot of mail about it.

474
00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:19.560
From Ross C., Sierra S., Allie E., and Emily D., did the candle room actually smell like

475
00:27:19.560 --> 00:27:20.140
candles?

476
00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:21.360
Oh, yeah.

477
00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:25.360
It was a room full of multiple scented candles.

478
00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:28.100
Well, I didn't have to go in the candle room.

479
00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:31.940
And thank goodness, because I was pregnant and so sensitive to smell, I probably would

480
00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:32.640
have been like, ah!

481
00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:35.620
But Paul Feig spoke about the candle room.

482
00:27:35.720 --> 00:27:36.860
And here's the audio clip.

483
00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:38.840
The candle room.

484
00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:43.740
We just knew that we wanted the candle room to be insane.

485
00:27:44.160 --> 00:27:47.500
And the big thing was like, when you're in there, it smelled crazy.

486
00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:49.380
And it was like, how do we do that?

487
00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:51.360
How do we put that across to an audience?

488
00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:55.240
Because one thing an audience can't take in, the two things they can't take in, the temperature,

489
00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:57.880
of what's happening, and they can't take in the smell of what's happening.

490
00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:01.660
And so that's why you have that shot of John Krasinski kind of like looking out the door

491
00:28:01.660 --> 00:28:06.320
and you're like, ooh, like he can't breathe, because you just know it just stinks in there.

492
00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:10.680
But I really just loved how dense and terrible he made that room.

493
00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:15.980
I loved John's look to camera when he was gasping for air.

494
00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:18.700
It was so believable and funny.

495
00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:23.580
And I don't think that was probably an easy thing to convey, but he did it so well.

496
00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:24.920
So well.

497
00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:27.980
John and I talked with Paul before we shot that scene.

498
00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:33.760
And he said, I want it to look like you guys are doing that thing when you walk into a stinky

499
00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:38.080
public restroom and you've taken like a deep breath before you walk in.

500
00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:40.520
You're trying to hold it for as long as you can.

501
00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:42.560
And then you need air.

502
00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:47.540
So you're just trying to take in the smallest amount of air that will get you through.

503
00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:48.460
Yeah.

504
00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:52.060
So that's what we were trying to do in that scene.

505
00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.900
Well, now they move into the master bedroom.

506
00:28:55.340 --> 00:28:56.200
Oh, my gosh.

507
00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:58.420
There are so many delicious moments in this.

508
00:28:58.580 --> 00:29:00.640
I do not know how you guys got through this.

509
00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:04.740
Well, I'm going to say that a lot through this episode because there are scenes that were just

510
00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:08.300
the four of you that are some of the funniest scenes I've ever seen.

511
00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:10.320
And I don't know how you got through this one.

512
00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:16.560
This is the scene where for the longest time, there's just a camera on a tripod pointed at

513
00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:16.980
the bed.

514
00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:17.740
Yeah.

515
00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:20.300
Which Jim and Pam clock immediately.

516
00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:21.700
And so does the audience.

517
00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:25.700
And then Jan says, Michael, I told you to put that away.

518
00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.420
And then she swats him on the butt.

519
00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:28.720
It's so uncomfortable.

520
00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.800
She swats him on the butt.

521
00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:32.360
Yeah.

522
00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:39.300
And then we learned that Michael sleeps on a bench at the foot of the bed.

523
00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:46.880
And Angela, I can't even tell you how hard we laughed every time Steve Carell curled up

524
00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:47.440
on that bench.

525
00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:48.620
When he was like, no, I fit.

526
00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:50.180
Look, it's actually pretty comfortable.

527
00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:52.640
He just curls up like a baby on it.

528
00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:56.580
Well, Paul shared with us the inspiration behind the bench.

529
00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:57.860
And it's so great.

530
00:29:58.100 --> 00:29:58.860
Here it is.

531
00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:06.280
So the whole thing with Michael and Jan and that weird bench that he sleeps on at the

532
00:30:06.280 --> 00:30:13.540
end of the bed came from when I was working as a script reader in the early 80s for a

533
00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.300
Hollywood, big Hollywood producer.

534
00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.280
And I befriended this woman who worked in this company.

535
00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:23.800
And at one point we were hanging out and we went by her house to pick something up.

536
00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:27.800
And I went in to there and they had this open bedroom plan.

537
00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:31.140
And I looked and there was this giant bed, like a king-sized bed.

538
00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.960
But then next to the bed was this little tiny cot.

539
00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:38.580
And I remember saying like, oh, what's that?

540
00:30:38.760 --> 00:30:40.180
And she got very serious.

541
00:30:40.300 --> 00:30:44.240
And she said like, look, when you get in a relationship, you have to make sure that you're

542
00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:49.080
able to be intimate with somebody and share things with them.

543
00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:57.500
And what it turned out was that when once they were done with their time together in bed, she

544
00:30:57.500 --> 00:31:03.600
had to roll off onto this cot and sleep by herself because this guy couldn't handle having

545
00:31:03.600 --> 00:31:05.540
anybody else in his bed with him.

546
00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:10.000
And I just thought that was the saddest thing I've ever seen in my life.

547
00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:14.880
And so I remember when we were scouting that location and there was that little stool at

548
00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:16.780
the end or that bench at the end of the bed.

549
00:31:17.320 --> 00:31:21.860
I can't remember if it was in the script or if we took it and ran with it.

550
00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:25.580
But I remember saying to Lee and Gene, like I told them that story.

551
00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:26.920
I said like, how funny.

552
00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:29.480
We have to have this thing where Michael can't sleep in the bed.

553
00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:32.520
And so that's what we did.

554
00:31:32.520 --> 00:31:37.340
And I just remember when we were shooting it, it made me laugh so much because, you know,

555
00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:39.940
the way that Steve Carell would curl up on that bench.

556
00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:44.020
And my whole thing was like, OK, we got to hang on this as long as we can.

557
00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:47.620
And so, you know, we have Jan going like, oh, he fits perfectly.

558
00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:48.160
Ha ha ha.

559
00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:54.280
And I just tried to get every last frame out of everyone just staring at Michael on this

560
00:31:54.280 --> 00:31:54.760
bench.

561
00:31:55.580 --> 00:31:56.740
And it's silent.

562
00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:58.960
And it's just completely uncomfortable.

563
00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:01.020
And it makes me laugh still to this day.

564
00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:13.040
Now, lady, that clip suggests that maybe this was the condo guy's bed and bench, because

565
00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:18.640
he said when they scouted the location, they saw the bench or maybe just a bench and then

566
00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:19.800
we recreated it.

567
00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:22.620
But I have a bench at the end of my bed.

568
00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:23.800
No one sleeps on it.

569
00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:25.740
But this is a very popular thing.

570
00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:29.540
I have an upholstered bench at the foot of my bed as well.

571
00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:31.740
It's where I like sort of lay my robe at night.

572
00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:33.240
You know, I like kick off my slippers.

573
00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:34.540
I put my robe on the bench.

574
00:32:34.760 --> 00:32:34.920
Yeah.

575
00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:36.460
And it's all there for me in the morning.

576
00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:39.820
My bench could not fit a grown man.

577
00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:41.880
It's a tiny little bench.

578
00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:46.660
I actually really looked at that bench and I was like, oh, they found a wide bench.

579
00:32:46.900 --> 00:32:48.340
That's a substantial bench.

580
00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:49.680
Yes, exactly.

581
00:32:50.120 --> 00:32:57.000
It's just big enough that you believe it, but still small enough that it's just so sad.

582
00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:57.520
Yeah.

583
00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:02.380
And also, just again, Michael has no space in his own home.

584
00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:08.960
She has both guest rooms she's using, and now he doesn't even get to sleep in his bed.

585
00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.400
It's so sad.

586
00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:11.580
It's tragic.

587
00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:13.720
And now I've got a fun fact.

588
00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:23.580
But this scene of the bedroom tour specifically, this scene is John McCain's favorite scene from the office.

589
00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:25.280
And I can tell you why.

590
00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:25.600
Yeah.

591
00:33:25.700 --> 00:33:26.400
How do you know that?

592
00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:28.840
That's such a random tidbit to know.

593
00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:32.600
BJ Novak was at a dinner party with John McCain.

594
00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:36.040
I would love to hear that story, how that came about.

595
00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:37.840
That's fascinating to me.

596
00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.700
That's very on brand, though, for BJ.

597
00:33:39.700 --> 00:33:43.880
BJ finds himself in these eclectic groups of people all the time.

598
00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:46.840
So this was, what, a few years ago, he's at a dinner party.

599
00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:47.900
John McCain's there.

600
00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:48.540
Yeah.

601
00:33:48.700 --> 00:33:52.960
You want to be BJ's plus one to a dinner party because he's going to take you to a cool group.

602
00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:53.400
Yeah.

603
00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:57.000
He was at this dinner party, and someone walked up to him and said,

604
00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:59.720
John McCain is a huge fan of the office.

605
00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:01.380
Would you like me to make an introduction?

606
00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:04.620
Now, I've experienced this, too, with politicians.

607
00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:10.720
A lot of times, politicians, they have a person whose job it is to whisper things in their ears

608
00:34:10.720 --> 00:34:16.520
that are really specific about the other guests at a party because they vetted everyone at the party

609
00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:22.080
so that when the politician meets you, they can say something really specific and personal.

610
00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:25.240
It's like the scene, you guys, in Devil Wears Prada, right?

611
00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:27.220
Anne Hathaway is next to Meryl Streep.

612
00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:32.140
They're at that big shindig in Paris, and she's telling her, every little person that walks up,

613
00:34:32.200 --> 00:34:32.980
some little tidbit.

614
00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:33.740
Yes.

615
00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:41.000
So BJ was like, oh, he's probably been told, I work on this show called The Office and yada yada.

616
00:34:41.180 --> 00:34:46.600
So BJ was like, I'm going to put him to the test, and I'm going to ask him specifically,

617
00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:51.800
John McCain, tell me one of your favorite scenes from The Office.

618
00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:52.460
BJ.

619
00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:53.760
Right?

620
00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:55.380
My gosh.

621
00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:55.840
Okay.

622
00:34:56.240 --> 00:34:58.620
So when he met him, he did it.

623
00:34:58.780 --> 00:35:01.040
He said, what's one of your favorite scenes?

624
00:35:01.040 --> 00:35:08.560
And John McCain said, I love that scene in the dinner party when they're given the house tour,

625
00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:13.900
and they walk into the bedroom, and the video camera's up in the background, and the bench.

626
00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:20.020
And he just went on and on with such great detail that BJ knew, oh, this is for real.

627
00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:21.520
This guy does love The Office.

628
00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:23.160
That's so wild.

629
00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:24.800
It's so specific.

630
00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:25.420
Yeah.

631
00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:27.040
It's such a specific scene.

632
00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:28.280
It was.

633
00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:32.640
Well, he picked a good episode, and he picked a good scene, because when I was re-watching this

634
00:35:32.640 --> 00:35:37.200
with my husband, Lee, this was the scene where I thought we were going to have to pause,

635
00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:38.820
because he couldn't catch his breath.

636
00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:40.640
He was, like, laughing so hard.

637
00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:43.960
All right.

638
00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:48.440
Well, speaking of having to, like, pause to catch your breath, because you're laughing so hard,

639
00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:52.900
Jenna, up next is the scene with the tiny TV.

640
00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:54.420
Oh, my God.

641
00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:59.020
The tiny plasma TV that only comes out two inches from the wall.

642
00:35:59.810 --> 00:36:05.780
There are two times when I laughed so hard on our show that I thought I wasn't going to ever

643
00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:06.260
recover.

644
00:36:07.540 --> 00:36:09.080
This was one of them.

645
00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:11.520
Tears streaming down our face.

646
00:36:11.520 --> 00:36:16.400
We had a fan question from Matty S, Paula T, Kennedy H.

647
00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:22.220
How many takes did you guys have to do when Michael was showing off his plasma TV?

648
00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:25.980
Guys, John Krasinski talks about this.

649
00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:27.680
I'm going to turn it over to him.

650
00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:35.040
When we got to the moment with the television, I think there was an energy in the room that was

651
00:36:35.040 --> 00:36:35.580
overwhelming.

652
00:36:35.780 --> 00:36:38.900
I was already quaking with laughter.

653
00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:41.340
It wouldn't have taken much for me to laugh.

654
00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:46.880
And then throw in maybe one of my favorite jokes in the entire show.

655
00:36:47.140 --> 00:36:49.740
And I remember at the table, read laughing.

656
00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:58.000
But then to see Steve again with that beaming face of pride, just turn to us and say,

657
00:36:58.120 --> 00:37:01.820
you won't believe what I have.

658
00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:06.580
And also, look what it can do when company comes over.

659
00:37:06.740 --> 00:37:08.040
I can just do this.

660
00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:16.240
And I'm pretty sure that the distance between the TV and the wall was like an inch and a half.

661
00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:21.480
And he was so proud that it comes out to watch TV in the comfort of your own home.

662
00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:28.240
And then you push it back a good inch and a half to hide it away when company comes.

663
00:37:28.540 --> 00:37:32.080
Luckily, the outtakes are out there in the world.

664
00:37:32.260 --> 00:37:35.120
And so you can see, I mean, Jenna can tell you.

665
00:37:35.260 --> 00:37:44.280
But I think that it took probably 19 or 20 takes before I was even able to stand up vertically

666
00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:47.600
in that scene without doubling over.

667
00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:50.440
And again, as I've always said, I'm a crier laugher.

668
00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:54.160
So when I laugh that hard, my entire face turns red with tears.

669
00:37:54.580 --> 00:38:00.380
And the pressure that's filling in my face for trying not to laugh and to no avail.

670
00:38:00.600 --> 00:38:03.000
I don't know why I even try not to laugh.

671
00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:04.320
I should just let it out.

672
00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:07.080
And then my face wouldn't turn beet red in every scene.

673
00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:10.400
That is without a doubt one of my favorite scenes.

674
00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:14.480
And I will cherish that memory for the rest of my career.

675
00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:20.440
You can hear him losing it, just retelling it, you know?

676
00:38:21.060 --> 00:38:21.420
Yes.

677
00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:27.680
Well, Jenna, one of my daughter's favorite things since we've been doing Office Ladies

